```java
class Queue {
    private Node front;
    private Node rear;

    public Queue() {
        this.front = null;
        this.rear = null;
    }

    public void enqueue(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (rear == null) {
            front = newNode;
            rear = newNode;
        } else {
            rear.next = newNode;
            rear = newNode;
        }
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        if (front == null) {
            return -1; // Queue is empty
        }
        int data = front.data;
        front = front.next;
        if (front == null) {
            rear = null; // Queue is now empty
        }
        return data;
    }

    public void printQueue() {
        Node current = front;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " ");
            current = current.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```
This implementation of the Queue class uses a linked list to simulate the queue data structure. The `enqueue` method adds elements to the end of the queue, the `dequeue` method removes elements from the front of the queue, and the `printQueue` method prints all the elements of the queue.

