        input_grad[i] = 0.0f;
        for (int j = 0; j < OUTPUT_SIZE; j++) {
            input_grad[i] += weights[j * INPUT_SIZE + i] * output_grad[j];
        }
    }

    // Calculate weights gradients
    for (int i = 0; i < OUTPUT_SIZE; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < INPUT_SIZE; j++) {
            weights_grad[i * INPUT_SIZE + j] = input[j] * output_grad[i];
        }
    }

    // Calculate bias gradients
    for (int i = 0; i < OUTPUT_SIZE; i++) {
        bias_grad[i] = output_grad[i];
    }
}
```
In the solution, the `backward_propagation` function calculates the gradients of the input, weights, and bias using the chain rule. The input gradients are computed by multiplying the weights with the output gradients. The weights gradients are computed by multiplying the input with the output gradients. The bias gradients are simply the output gradients.

